## 8. Notion Database Integration

Notion must be the single source of truth.

The implementation must support retrieving records from a Notion database and transforming them into a consistent internal data model.

### Important: Inspect the actual Notion schema

Do not assume that my existing Notion database uses exactly the property names or structures described in this prompt.

Before implementing the synchronisation script:

1. Inspect the actual Notion database schema through the Notion API.
2. Identify available properties and their types.
3. Map those properties to the internal book data model.
4. Create a configurable property-mapping layer.
5. Document missing or unsupported properties.
6. Avoid silently discarding available information.

If a required property is missing, explain what needs to be added or mapped.

Do not modify the Notion database automatically.

### Supported book status values

Reading status:

- DNF
- Reading
- To Be Read
- Read
- Wishlist

Format:

- Paperback
- Hardback
- Audiobook
- Ebook/Kindle

These values should be supported exactly, including the ability to add more values later.

If the actual Notion database uses different labels, map them through configuration rather than requiring me to rename every record.

### Suggested internal data model

Use a consistent internal structure similar to:

```json
{
  "id": "unique-book-id",
  "slug": "example-book-title",
  "notion_page_id": "notion-page-id",
  "title": "Example Book",
  "author": "Example Author",
  "cover_url": "https://example.com/cover.jpg",
  "description": "Book description",
  "language": "English",
  "original_language": "Arabic",
  "publication_year": 2024,
  "publisher": "Example Publisher",
  "isbn": "9780000000000",
  "pages": 320,
  "format": "Paperback",
  "status": "Read",
  "current_page": null,
  "rating": 5,
  "favourite": false,
  "genres": [
    "Literary Fiction",
    "Historical Fiction"
  ],
  "date_added": "2026-01-01",
  "date_started": "2026-01-02",
  "date_finished": "2026-01-15",
  "review": "Public review, if available",
  "goodreads_url": "https://www.goodreads.com/"
}
```

This is an example structure, not a requirement to force every property into Notion.

Use null or omit unavailable values appropriately.

### Data handling requirements

- Support multiple genres per book.
- Support missing covers.
- Support missing descriptions.
- Support books without ratings.
- Support incomplete reading dates.
- Support different reading formats.
- Support books with no page count.
- Support multiple books by the same author.
- Support duplicate detection.
- Preserve stable identifiers.
- Normalise text values consistently.

A missing value must not break the website.

